\subsection{Boundary Cases and Scale Effect}
\label{sec:boundary_extended}

Of 9 continuation-mode configurations on C4, 7 yield $\Kempirical{=}2$; Gemma-2-2B and Mistral-7B are competence-limited at $\Kempirical{=}1$ (1v2 $\auc$: 0.598, 0.594 respectively), validating $\theta{=}0.60$ as an effective discriminator (gap: $0.594$--$0.598$ vs.\ $0.611$--$0.654$).
Mistral's cross-domain profile reinforces the domain-governed interpretation: despite $\Kempirical{=}1$ on C4 and Wikipedia, it yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ on code and $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv (1v2 $\auc = 0.820$, 2v3 $= 0.628$), and this profile persists under cross-scorer validation (Pythia-1.4B scorer: C4 $\Kempirical{=}1$, arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.608$, CI $[0.593, 0.622]$)), domain lexical diversity can therefore rescue even web-text one-wash generators (Table~\ref{tab:domain_kstar}).
Larger models produce more human-like text, reducing 0v1 JSD: 0.16--0.29 at $\leq$1.5B, 0.077--0.13 at 7--14B.
However, Qwen-14B still yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.621$, CI $[0.612, 0.630]$), with 0v1 $\auc = 0.960$ comparable to Qwen-7B ($0.964$), scale in the 1B--14B range therefore does not shift the discrimination boundary.

\paragraph{Sample-size control.}
As a sample-size control, subsampling C4 to $n{=}2{,}200$ (matching arXiv) yields $\Kempirical{=}1$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.601$, CI $[0.586, 0.616]$).
Reduced sample size widens confidence intervals and produces more conservative $\Kempirical$ estimates, arXiv's $\Kempirical{=}3$ at the same sample size therefore reflects genuine domain signal rather than a statistical artifact of smaller training sets.

\subsection{LaTeX Markup Ablation (arXiv)}
\label{sec:latex_ablation}

To disentangle content-level depth signal from formatting artifacts on arXiv, we strip all LaTeX markup (commands, environments, inline math delimiters) and re-extract features from the plain-text residual (Qwen-1.5B continuation, $n{=}2{,}200$; Table~\ref{tab:latex_ablation}).

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\caption{arXiv pairwise $\auc$ with and without LaTeX markup (Qwen-1.5B continuation). Removing formatting reduces 2v3 by ${\sim}$1.8\,pp but $\Kempirical$ remains at 3 (CI lower $> \theta$). LaTeX stripping fraction increases with depth (0.6\% at depth 0, 4.6\% at depth 5), deeper generations therefore produce proportionally more formatting tokens.}
\label{tab:latex_ablation}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccr}
\toprule
\textbf{Pair} & \textbf{Original} & \textbf{Stripped} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
0v1 & 0.9946 & 0.9948 & $+$0.0002 \\
1v2 & 0.8580 & 0.8297 & $-$0.028 \\
2v3 & 0.6427 $[0.628,\,0.658]$ & 0.6248 $[0.610,\,0.640]$ & $-$0.018 \\
3v4 & 0.5437 & 0.5468 & $+$0.003 \\
\midrule
$\Kempirical$ & 3 & 3 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Content-level statistical properties (surprisal distribution, vocabulary diversity) account for the large majority of the depth signal; LaTeX formatting contributes a modest but measurable increment (${\sim}$1.8\,pp at the boundary pair).
The depth-correlated increase in LaTeX stripping fraction (depth 0: $-$0.6\%, depth 5: $-$4.6\%) indicates that autoregressive generation progressively introduces formatting tokens, consistent with mode-seeking behavior in structured text.

\subsection{Statistical Power Analysis}
\label{sec:power_curve}

To characterize the minimum sample size required for reliable $\Kempirical$ determination, we subsample C4 at $n \in \{500, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000\}$ and measure 1v2 AUC stability across 10 resamples per size (Qwen-1.5B-base continuation, 5-fold GroupKFold; Table~\ref{tab:power_curve}, Figure~\ref{fig:power_curve}).

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\caption{Subsampling power analysis for $\Kempirical{=}2$ determination (C4, Qwen-1.5B-base, 10 resamples per $n$). Detection probability: fraction of resamples where 1v2 $\auc > \theta{=}0.60$.}
\label{tab:power_curve}
\begin{tabular}{rccc}
\toprule
$n$ & \textbf{Mean 1v2 $\auc$} & \textbf{Std} & \textbf{Detection Prob.} \\
\midrule
500 & 0.581 & 0.018 & 20\% \\
1{,}000 & 0.591 & 0.014 & 30\% \\
2{,}000 & 0.610 & 0.009 & 100\% \\
3{,}000 & 0.617 & 0.005 & 100\% \\
4{,}000 & 0.622 & 0.004 & 100\% \\
5{,}000 & 0.629 & 0.000 & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_power_curve.png}
\caption{$\Kempirical{=}2$ detection rate (left axis, blue) and mean 1v2 $\auc$ (right axis, green) as a function of sample size $n$. Detection rate reaches 100\% at $n{=}2{,}000$; the 80\% power threshold (dotted purple) is crossed between $n{=}1{,}000$ and $n{=}2{,}000$. Error bars: $\pm$1 std over 10 resamples. The dashed red line marks $\theta{=}0.60$.}
\label{fig:power_curve}
\end{figure}

Detection rate jumps from 20\% ($n{=}500$) to 100\% ($n{=}2{,}000$), with the 80\% power threshold crossed near $n{=}1{,}500$.
At $n{=}5{,}000$ (the sample size used in all main experiments), variance is zero across resamples, our results are therefore well above the minimum required for reliable boundary estimation.
The sharp transition reflects the proximity of the 1v2 AUC to $\theta$: small increases in $n$ reduce variance sufficiently to move all resamples above the threshold.

\subsection{Feature Group Ablation}
\label{sec:feature_ablation}

To assess the relative contribution of each feature group to depth discrimination, we train a plain LightGBM classifier (\texttt{n\_estimators}$=$200, \texttt{max\_depth}$=$6; distinct from the OBD-LightGBM used in Section~\ref{sec:method}) on the 1\,vs.\,2 task using (a) each feature group alone (\emph{single}) and (b) the full 15D set minus one group (\emph{leave-one-out}).
Representative models per domain: LLaMA-3.1-8B (C4), Qwen-1.5B continuation (arXiv), and the domain ablation configuration (Wikipedia).

